WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.500
Today, it is almost taken for granted that
neural network-based learning algorithms are

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:15.929
capable of identifying objects in images,
or even write full, coherent sentences about

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:21.000
them, but fewer people know that there is
also parallel research on trying to break

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
these systems.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.030
For instance, some of these image detectors
can be fooled by adding a little noise to

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:32.489
the image, and in some specialized cases,
we can even perform something that is called

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:34.239
the one pixel attack.

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:37.019
Let’s have a look at some examples.

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:43.570
Changing just this one pixel can make a classifier
think that this ship is a car, or that this

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:49.469
horse is a frog, and amusingly, be quite confident
about its guess.

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:55.170
Note that the choice of this pixel and the
color is by no means random and it needs solving

00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:00.649
a mathematical optimization problem to find
out exactly how to perform this.

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:04.940
Trying to build better image detectors, while
other researchers are trying to break them

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.150
is not the only arms race we’re experiencing
in machine learning research.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:15.160
For instance, a few years ago, DeepMind introduced
an incredible learning algorithm that looked

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:21.230
at the screen, much like a human would, but
was able to reach superhuman levels in playing

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.660
a few Atari games.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.670
It was a spectacular milestone in AI research.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.620
They also have just published a followup paper
on this that we’ll cover very soon, so make

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.230
sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon to
not miss it when it appears in the near future.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.080
Interestingly, while these learning algorithms
are being improved at a staggering pace, there

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.290
is a parallel subfield where researchers endeavor
to break these learning systems by slightly

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.380
changing the information they are presented
with.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.940
Let’s have a look at OpenAI’s example.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:58.350
Their first method adds a tiny bit of noise
to a large portion of the video input, where

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:03.500
the difference is barely perceptible, but
it forces the learning algorithm to choose

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.950
a different action than it would have chosen
otherwise.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.270
In the other one, a different modification
was used, that has a smaller footprint, but

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.270
is more visible.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:20.050
For instance, in pong, adding a tiny fake
ball to the game can coerce the learner into

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.930
going down when it was originally planning
to go up.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.829
It is important to emphasize that the researchers
did not do this by hand.

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:35.630
The algorithm itself is able to pick up game-specific
knowledge by itself and find out how to fool

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.730
the other AI using it.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.430
Both attacks perform remarkably well.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.930
However, it is not always true that we can
just change these images or the playing environment

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.499
to our desire to fool these algorithms.

00:02:48.499 --> 00:02:53.140
So, with this, an even more interesting question
arises.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:58.499
Is it possible to just enter the game as a
player, and perform interesting stunts that

00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:01.590
can reliably win against these AIs?

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:05.730
And with this, we have arrived to the subject
of today’s paper.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:10.079
This is the “You Shall Not Pass” game,
where the red agent is trying to hold back

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:13.980
the blue character and not let it cross the
line.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:19.920
Here you see two regular AIs duking it out,
sometimes the red wins, sometimes the blue

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.739
is able to get through.

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:23.379
Nothing too crazy here.

00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:27.159
This is the reference case which is somewhat
well balanced.

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:32.959
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
adversarial agent that this new paper proposes,

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:35.260
does this.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.239
You may think this was some kind of glitch,
and I put the incorrect footage here by accident.

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:46.189
No, this is not an error, you can believe
your eyes, it basically collapses and does

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:48.650
absolutely nothing.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.459
This can’t be a useful strategy, can it?

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:53.230
Well, look at that!

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.930
It still wins the majority of the time.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.560
This is very confusing.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.849
How can that be?

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:00.680
Let’s have a closer look.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:06.400
This red agent is normally a somewhat competent
player, as you can see here, it can punch

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.659
the blue victim and make it fall.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:15.980
We now replaced this red player with the adversarial
agent, which collapsed, and it almost feels

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.370
like it hypnotized the blue agent to also
fall.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.440
And now, squeeze your papers, because the
normal red opponent’s winrate was 47% percent,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.900
and this collapsing chap wins 86% of the time.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:36.750
It not only wins, but it wins much, much more
reliably than a competent AI.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.780
What is this wizardry?

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.780
The answer is that the adversary induces off-distribution
activations.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.169
To understand what that exactly means, let’s
have a look at this chart.

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:53.639
This tells us how likely it is that the actions
of the AI against different opponents are

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:54.639
normal.

00:04:54.639 --> 00:05:00.129
As you see, when this agent named Zoo plays
against itself, the bars are in the positive

00:05:00.129 --> 00:05:04.060
region, meaning that normal things are happening.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.620
Things go as expected.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:10.550
However, that’s not the case for the blue
lines, which are the actions when we play

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.699
against this adversarial agent, in which case,
the blue victim’s actions are not normal

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.169
in the slightest.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:23.539
So, the adversarial agent is really doing
nothing, but it is doing nothing in a way

00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:28.699
that reprograms its opponent to make mistakes
and behave close to a completely randomly

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:29.900
acting agent!

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.740
This paper is absolute insanity.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.590
I love it!

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:39.020
And if you look here, you see that the more
the blue curve improves, the better this scheme

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.500
works for a given game.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:47.400
For instance, it is doing real good on Kick
and Defend, fairly good on Sumo Humans, and

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.600
that there is something about the Sumo Ants
game that prevents this interesting kind of

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.080
hypnosis from happening.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.280
I’d love to see a followup paper that can
pull this off a little more reliably.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:11.259
What

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:38.750
a 
time

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:52.770
to be alive!

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.850
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

